{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "5",
    "case_summary": "A 40-year-old previously healthy man was admitted after 1 week of progressive apathy, unresponsiveness, and immobility. He became bedridden and required a feeding tube and urinary catheter. On transfer, he was in a vegetative state.  \nVital signs: temperature 36.5 °C, heart rate 80 bpm, blood pressure 135/78 mmHg, Glasgow Coma Scale 7 (E3 V1 M3). Pupils 3.5 mm, briskly reactive; corneal reflexes present. Both axial and appendicular muscles were rigid, with flexed arms and extended legs; bilateral Babinski and Chaddock signs were positive.  \nLaboratory studies: WBC 3.79×10^9/L, neutrophils 74%, CRP 6.07 mg/L, ESR 21 mm/h; HIV, syphilis, hepatitis B and C serologies negative; ANA, anticardiolipin, ANCA, and T-spot negative. Liver and renal functions were normal.  \nLumbar puncture: opening pressure 50 mmH₂O; CSF clear, protein 3.64 g/L, glucose 2.6 mmol/L (blood glucose 5.38 mmol/L), chloride 123 mmol/L, leukocytes 0/mm³. CSF India ink stain and bacterial cultures were negative. CSF oligoclonal bands, AQP4, MOG, and GFAP antibodies were negative; CSF fungal cultures were negative.  \nImaging: Brain CT showed a hypodense lesion in the corpus callosum. MRI revealed a homogeneous mass in the corpus callosum body, hypointense on T1, isointense on T2 FLAIR, with rim enhancement and surrounding vasogenic edema on postcontrast T1. DWI demonstrated rim hyperintensity but no central restricted diffusion.",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Multisystem Syndrome with Altered Mental Status and Motor Rigidity",
